    "faq_section_bjxzKR": {
      "type": "faq-section",
      "blocks": {
        "collapsible_row_4VGV9b": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Is Wegovy safe? ",
            "row_content": "<p>Wegovy has been extensively studied and is licensed in the UK for effective weight management in adults. It is approved by NICE (the National Institute for Health and Care Excellence) and is one of the most clinically researched weight-loss medications available. <\/p><p>As with all medications, it isn't suitable for everyone — which is why every settlehealth prescription follows a thorough clinical assessment. The most commonly reported side effects are gastrointestinal — including nausea and digestive discomfort — and tend to settle as your body adjusts. Your clinician will review your full health history before prescribing and will support you throughout your treatment.<\/p>"
          }
        },
        "collapsible_row_bzzp7L": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "How long does Wegovy take to work? ",
            "row_content": "<p>Most people begin to notice a difference in appetite and food cravings within the first few weeks of starting Wegovy. Weight-loss changes typically become visible between four and eight weeks, with more significant results building over three to six months. <\/p><p>Wegovy is introduced gradually — starting at a lower dose and increasing over sixteen to twenty weeks — to allow your body to adjust and minimise side effects. The results build with consistency over time, and your clinician will support you through every stage of that process.<\/p>"
          }
        },
        "collapsible_row_C9p89w": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What is the difference between Wegovy and Mounjaro ? ",
            "row_content": "<p>Both are clinically proven weight-loss medications that are injected weekly. But they work differently. Wegovy contains semaglutide, which mimics the single hormone glucagon-like peptide (GLP-1) to reduce cravings and help you feel fuller for longer. Mounjaro contains tirzepatide, which targets the two hormones GLP-1 and glucose-dependent insulinotropic polypeptide (GIP) simultaneously to slow digestion, reduce appetite and help you feel fuller for longer. <\/p><p>Individual results vary with both medications. The right choice depends on your health history, your body and your goals. And that’s what we’re here for. Your clinician will assess this during your consultation to find the most effective medication for you. <\/p>"
          }
        },
        "collapsible_row_VqL7ez": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
